; Test: COUNT explained - what does it actually do?
; ==================================================

; Setup: Three colors
(color apple red)
(color banana yellow)
(color grape purple)

; Question: What does COUNT count?
;
; Syntax: (count <report-template> $k <template-to-count>)
;
; The third argument (<template-to-count>) is NOT a pattern to match!
; It's a template that gets instantiated with the bound variables from the exec.

; Test 1: Count matches and see what template does
(exec (0 test1)
  (, (color $fruit $c))
  (O (+ (matched $fruit $c))
     (count (total $k) $k (counted $fruit $c))))

; What happens:
; 1. Input pattern (, (color $fruit $c)) matches 3 times:
;    - $fruit=apple, $c=red
;    - $fruit=banana, $c=yellow
;    - $fruit=grape, $c=purple
;
; 2. For EACH match, the template (counted $fruit $c) is instantiated:
;    - (counted apple red)
;    - (counted banana yellow)
;    - (counted grape purple)
;
; 3. COUNT collects all UNIQUE instantiations in its internal PathMap
;
; 4. COUNT returns the NUMBER of unique instantiations → 3
;
; 5. Report template (total $k) becomes (total 3)

; Expected output:
; (matched apple red)
; (matched banana yellow)
; (matched grape purple)
; (total 3)
;
; Note: The template "counted" is just a dummy symbol.
; COUNT never adds (counted ...) facts to the space!
; It only uses them internally to count unique instances.
